<article class="ltx_document" lang="en">
<h1 class="ltx_title ltx_title_document">LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade</h1>
<div class="ltx_authors">
<span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Aida Kostikova
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Bielefeld University
</span>
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_email"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Email: </span><a href="mailto:%5B">[</a>
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Ole Pütz
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Bielefeld University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Steffen Eger
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>University of Technology Nuremberg
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Olga Sabelfeld
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Bielefeld University
</span></span></span></span>
<span class="ltx_author_before">  </span><span class="ltx_creator ltx_role_author">
<span class="ltx_personname">Benjamin Paassen
</span><span class="ltx_author_notes"><span class="ltx_author_notes_content">
<span class="ltx_contact ltx_role_affiliation"><span class="ltx_contact_name">Affiliation: </span>Bielefeld University
</span></span></span></span></div>

<div id="abstract1" class="ltx_abstract"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_abstract">Abstract</h6>
    
<p id="abstract1.1" class="ltx_p">Migration has been a core topic in German political debate, from millions of expellees post World War II over labor migration to refugee movements in the recent past. Studying political speech regarding such wide-ranging phenomena in depth traditionally required extensive manual annotations, limiting the scope of analysis to small subsets of the data. Large language models (LLMs) have the potential to partially automate even complex annotation tasks. We provide an extensive evaluation of a multiple LLMs in annotating (anti-)solidarity subtypes in German parliamentary debates compared to a large set of thousands of human reference annotations (gathered over a year). We evaluate the influence of model size, prompting differences, fine-tuning, historical versus contemporary data; and we investigate systematic errors. Beyond methodological evaluation, we also interpret the resulting annotations from a social science lense, gaining deeper insight into (anti-)solidarity trends towards migrants in the German post-World War II period and recent past. Our data reveals a high degree of migrant-directed solidarity in the postwar period, as well as a strong trend towards anti-solidarity in the German parliament since 2015, motivating further research. These findings highlight the promise of LLMs for political text analysis and the importance of migration debates in Germany, where demographic decline and labor shortages coexist with rising polarization.</p>
  
</div>
<div class="ltx_classification"><h6 class="ltx_title ltx_title_classification">keywords</h6>solidarity, large language models, annotation, political discourse
</div>
<div id="p1" class="ltx_para"><span id="p1.1" class="ltx_ERROR undefined">\cup@datesfalse</span>
<p id="p1.2" class="ltx_p">Aida Kostikova]aida.kostikova@uni-bielefeld.de</p>
</div>
<section id="S1" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">1 </span>Introduction</h2>

<div id="S1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Migration is a defining feature of the 21st century, explicitly recognized in the 2030 Agenda for Sustainable Development <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx104" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wilkinson et al., 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Adger et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. As the <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx105" title="" class="ltx_ref">World Bank (2023)</a></cite> stresses, global economic imbalances, demographic divergence, and climate change make migration increasingly unavoidable, especially from the Global South to the Global North <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx3" title="" class="ltx_ref">Almulhim et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx55" title="" class="ltx_ref">Korsi, 2022</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx68" title="" class="ltx_ref">Marois et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. However, if governed responsibly, migration can also drive prosperity and contribute to achieving the Sustainable Development Goals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx105" title="" class="ltx_ref">World Bank, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx38" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gavonel et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx1" title="" class="ltx_ref">Adger et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Within Europe, Germany occupies a particularly important position as a major migration destination <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx32" title="" class="ltx_ref">European Commission, Eurostat, 2024</a>)</cite>. As the EU’s largest economy, it offers labor market opportunities, institutional support, and stability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx10" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bertoli et al., 2016</a>)</cite>. At the same time, demographic decline and skilled-labor shortages make sustained immigration economically beneficial <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx27" title="" class="ltx_ref">Donal &amp; Huang, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx29" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eissel et al., 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx4" title="" class="ltx_ref">Angenendt et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. Migration has also profoundly shaped Germany’s political development, from the resettlement of millions of expellees after WWII, to the labor migration of the 1950s–1970s, to refugee movements in the past decade <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx52" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kaya, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fröhlich, 2025</a>)</cite>. This raises important questions: how does German politics approach migration, and to what extent does it express solidarity – or anti-solidarity – toward migrants?</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Answering such questions requires systematic analysis of political discourse across long time spans, traditionally relying on
manual textual analysis and qualitative interpretation, which remain powerful tools <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx77" title="" class="ltx_ref">Randour et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. However, such methods are labor-intensive <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx11" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bhattacherjee, 2012</a>)</cite> and thus often restricted to selective subsets of data, which risks sampling bias and leaves large corpora underutilized <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx7" title="" class="ltx_ref">Babalola &amp; Nwanzu, 2021</a>)</cite>. Computational social science (CSS) emerged to address the scale and complexity of modern data, yet it often struggles with theoretical alignment and interpretive depth <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx61" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lazer &amp; Ognyanova, 2024</a>)</cite>. Large language models (LLMs) promise to bridge this gap by enabling automated, in-depth analysis of large-scale text. While LLMs have shown strong performance on tasks like ideological stance detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziems et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, it remains an open question whether they can handle more complex, theory-driven sociopolitical annotation tasks. We provide a partial answer: <em id="S1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">If accompanied by substantial validation and careful interpretation, LLMs can approximate the performance of trained human annotators and generate annotations that help quantify large-scale trends of complex constructs.</em> In particular, we apply LLMs to annotate solidarity and anti-solidarity towards women and migrants in German parliamentary speeches, compare LLM annotations to thousands of human reference annotations, and interpret the (anti-)solidarity trends between 1949-1969 and 2009-2025 in light of German political history.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S1.F1" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07274v1/SolidarityAnnotationScheme.png" id="S1.F1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:429/117;" width="429" height="117" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S1.F1.13" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 1</span>: </span><span id="S1.F1.14" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Overview of the annotation scheme adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen (2012)</a></cite>. Statements are first classified at a high level as expressing <em id="S1.F1.14.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em>, <em id="S1.F1.14.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em>, <em id="S1.F1.14.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed</em>, and <em id="S1.F1.14.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">none</em>. <em id="S1.F1.14.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Solidarity</em> and <em id="S1.F1.14.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em> instances are then further categorized into subtypes based on the underlying rationale: <em id="S1.F1.14.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based</em>, <em id="S1.F1.14.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exchange-based</em>, <em id="S1.F1.14.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate</em>, and <em id="S1.F1.14.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empathic</em>. The example sentence in the figure illustrates exchange-based anti-solidarity.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We focus on solidarity as a particularly demanding test case for such an approach.</span> While frequently invoked in political discourse, solidarity has often been treated as a symbolic notion rather than an operational concept <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx58" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kozłowska, 2024</a>)</cite>, which has limited large-scale empirical research. To address this gap, we approach solidarity as a socially meaningful category that can be systematically studied in political text. For this purpose, we draw on Thijssen’s typology of solidarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen, 2012</a>)</cite>, which provides a structured and annotatable framework. It distinguishes four types of (anti-)solidarity – group-based, compassionate, exchange-based, and empathic (see the annotation scheme in Figure <a href="#S1.F1" title="Figure 1 ‣ 1 Introduction ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a>) – based on the interplay of structural conditions (e.g., group membership) and intersubjective recognition (e.g., emotional identification). <span id="S1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Germany offers a particularly suitable context for such a study: solidarity is deeply rooted in its political vocabulary <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx34" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fiegle, 2003</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx93" title="" class="ltx_ref">Stjernø, 2005</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx83" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reisz, 2006</a>)</cite>, and its multi-party system enables us to trace how meanings of (anti-)solidarity vary across parties and over time.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In a prior conference paper, we already performed a first analysis of LLM annotations of (anti-)solidarity towards women and migrants on this data set <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In this paper, we 1) evaluate a much wider range of LLMs, 2) systematically evaluate the effect of prompts, model size, and historic (versus contemporary) data, 3) include a much larger data set of human annotations, 4) <span id="S1.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">validate robustness by comparing the time series of model-predicted (anti-)solidarity shares from the selected model (Llama-3.3-70B) and an independent model (GPT-4)</span>, and 5) most importantly, we interpret the (anti-)solidarity trends towards migrants over time to derive novel insights for political science. In particular, we find a) that different groups of migrants are associated with different types of (anti-)solidarity in German parliamentary debates during the post-World War II period; and b) that anti-solidarity towards migrants has dramatically increased in the German parliament over the past 10 years, indicating a shift to the right (especially in the liberal and conservative parties) on this issue that motivates further research.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">1.1 </span>Related Work</h3>

<div id="S1.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Our work builds on and connects the following directions of existing research: (i) sociological and political theories of solidarity and their operationalization in NLP; and (ii) the use of LLMs as annotation tools in political and social science contexts. While prior studies have engaged with each of these dimensions individually, few have combined them to enable large-scale, longitudinal analysis of solidarity in political speech. We briefly review these literatures below.</p>
</div>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Solidarity in Political Discourse and NLP</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Sociological theories of solidarity often begin with Durkheim’s classical distinction between mechanical and organic forms, which describe different bases for social cohesion. While influential, this binary <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">model</span> has been criticized for its structuralism and linear view of social change. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen (2012)</a></cite> expands on Durkheim by integrating recognition-based theories <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">such as those developed by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx45" title="" class="ltx_ref">Honneth (1996)</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx46" title="" class="ltx_ref">Honneth (2014)</a></cite></span> and proposing a fourfold typology with group-based, compassionate, exchange-based, and empathic subtypes, <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">which can be applied to both historical and contemporary political discourse.</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen &amp; Verheyen (2022)</a></cite> apply this typology to Belgian party manifestos, showing how different parties emphasize different solidarity frames in line with broader ideological divides. We build on this by applying the typology to parliamentary debates, extending the temporal scope and scaling the analysis with LLMs. While some studies have examined solidarity longitudinally using interviews or survey data <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx88" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schönweitz et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx91" title="" class="ltx_ref">Sengupta et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, these approaches are not designed for large-scale text analysis or fine-grained typological classification.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">At the same time, solidarity has received relatively little attention in computational text analysis, <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">where more application-driven concerns such as hate speech and misinformation detection have dominated the field.</span> Recent NLP studies have examined expressions of solidarity in social media during crisis events, such as refugee movements <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx102" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weber et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx28" title="" class="ltx_ref">Eger et al., 2022</a>)</cite>, the COVID-19 pandemic <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx51" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kalaloi et al., 2021</a>)</cite>, and natural disasters <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx87" title="" class="ltx_ref">Santhanam et al., 2019</a>)</cite>. These works typically use supervised models to detect solidarity or anti-solidarity in large-scale Twitter data and track how such expressions evolve over short timeframes. While they demonstrate the feasibility of automated solidarity detection, they focus on specific, time-bound crises and treat solidarity as a binary sentiment-like category rather than a differentiated moral frame. In contrast, our work applies a theory-driven typology to political speech, enabling fine-grained analysis of solidarity in parliamentary discourse.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">LLMs as Annotation Tools in Political and Social Science.</h5>

<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">LLMs are increasingly used in political and social science research. Most applications focus on standard classification tasks such as <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">political</span> ideology detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(e.g. <a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heseltine &amp; Clemm, 2024</a>)</cite>; sentiment analysis <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx60" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lashitew &amp; Mu, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx37" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fu et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; stance detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(e.g. <a href="#bib.bibx19" title="" class="ltx_ref">Cao et al., 2024</a>)</cite>; and misinformation detection <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(e.g. <a href="#bib.bibx47" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. For broader surveys of LLM applications, benefits, and challenges in social and political science, see <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx94" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thapa et al. (2025)</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx65" title="" class="ltx_ref">Li et al. (2024)</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziems et al. (2024)</a></cite>. However, conclusions about LLM performance in these fields vary. <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Some studies report that LLMs show near-human performance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx86" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rytting et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or even outperform humans, as well as traditional models, on short texts and tasks like ideology classification <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx62" title="" class="ltx_ref">Le &amp; Gallego, 2025</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heseltine &amp; Clemm, 2024</a>)</cite>.</span>
In contrast, several studies show that smaller fine-tuned models like BERT still outperform LLMs, especially when labeled data is available <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx18" title="" class="ltx_ref">Burnham et al., 2024</a>)</cite> or when tasks require expert-defined taxonomies and contextual reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziems et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. Our work provides a systemic evaluation of LLMs for such a complex, theory-informed task, namely (anti-)solidarity detection.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Beyond standard classification tasks,</span> a subset of research has applied language models to more complex, theory-driven categories. For example,
<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erhard et al. (2025)</a></cite> develop PopBERT, a fine-tuned transformer trained to detect populist rhetoric and ideological dimensions in <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">German</span> Bundestag speeches using a custom annotation scheme. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Card et al. (2022)</a></cite> analyze 140 years of U.S. political speech with fine-tuned RoBERTa and BERT models to classify immigration tone, identify rhetorical frames, and detect dehumanizing metaphors, demonstrating the utility of fine-tuned LMs for large-scale, theory-aligned analysis. <span id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Related work has applied fine-tuned models to pragmatic and rhetorical categories in German parliamentary debates, such as speaker attribution, speech acts, and opinion role labelling <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx82" title="" class="ltx_ref">Reinig et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx80" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rehbein et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx79" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rehbein &amp; Ponzetto, 2024</a>)</cite>.</span> In contrast to fine-tuned approaches, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjit et al. (2024)</a></cite> use general-purpose LLMs (e.g., GPT-4, Flan-T5) to apply a custom typology of attitudes toward homelessness, developed from framing theory and grounded theory. Their evaluation focuses on aggregate trends and correlations with external structural indicators, using LLMs to annotate a large corpus of tweets at scale and over time.</p>
</div>
<div id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S1.SS1.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Our work differs in several important ways. Unlike <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx30" title="" class="ltx_ref">Erhard et al. (2025)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx20" title="" class="ltx_ref">Card et al. (2022)</a></cite>, we use state-of-the-art LLMs and avoid the need for supervised data sets and fine-tuning. Unlike <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx78" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ranjit et al. (2024)</a></cite>, who also use prompted LLMs, we adopt a typology grounded in established sociological theory rather than one developed inductively for a specific domain. We apply this typology to more formal political speech rather than short, informal social media texts, and evaluate model performance through systematic comparison with human annotations. Finally, our analysis spans over 150 years of parliamentary debate, encompassing two case studies, historical and contemporary, and enabling fine-grained, longitudinal insight into political discourse.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S2" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">2 </span>Methodology</h2>

<section id="S2.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.1 </span>Data</h3>

<div id="S2.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We build on the DeuParl dataset introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walter et al. (2021)</a></cite>, which consists of plenary protocols from the German <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reichstag</span> (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: imperial diet) (sourced from the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Reichstagsprotokolle</span>) and the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bundestag</span> (<span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: federal parliament) (via the <span id="S2.SS1.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Open Data</span> platform), spanning 1867–2022. We extend this corpus to include protocols up to June 2025. We follow the preprocessing steps from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx100" title="" class="ltx_ref">Walter et al. (2021)</a></cite> and include metadata such as date, session number, and legislative period.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We reuse the keyword sets (see a full list of keywords in <a href="#A1" title="Appendix A List of Keywords ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a>) for the categories <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> (<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: migrant) and <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Frau</span> (<span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: woman) introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al. (2024)</a></cite>, consisting of 32 and 18 terms respectively. Based on these keywords, we extract 63k migrant-related and 138k women-related sentences (<em id="S2.SS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">instances</em>) from DeuParl, additionally including three sentences before and after each instance for context. We enrich each instance with speaker and party information, using <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Open Data</span> metadata for recent sessions (20th–21st periods) and improved XML markup for earlier ones (1st–19th) provided by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx76" title="" class="ltx_ref">Porada (2019)</a></cite>. See <a href="#A2" title="Appendix B Parties ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">B</span></a> for details.
Fig. <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Data ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> shows the number of instances over time, as well as their share relative to all sentences in the DeuParl dataset. <span id="S2.SS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Mentions of both migrants and women increase substantially after 1990, with migrant-related speech peaking and briefly overtaking that of women around 2015, coinciding with refugee crisis debates. Notably, migrant mentions also surpass women-related discourse in the immediate post-war years.</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F2" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/SentencesPerYearAndCategory.svg" id="S2.F2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/134;" width="457" height="134"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F2.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 2</span>: </span><span id="S2.F2.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Absolute number of instances (left) and their share relative to all DeuParl sentences (right) for the <span id="S2.F2.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Woman</span> and <span id="S2.F2.6.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Migrant</span> categories.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.2 </span>Data Annotation</h3>

<div id="S2.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">To validate our models and enable further qualitative analysis, we build on the human-annotated dataset introduced in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al. (2024)</a></cite>, which comprises 2,864 instances (1,437 for migrants, 1,427 for women) from the DeuParl corpus.<span id="footnote1" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">1</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">1</span>
              
              
              
            Annotation was carried out by five trained student assistants through an iterative process, achieving Cohen’s <math id="footnote1.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> of 0.62 (high level) and 0.42 (fine-grained). Final labels in the dataset are assigned by majority vote.</span></span></span> Each instance is labeled according to a fine-grained solidarity framing scheme that distinguishes four high-level stance categories – <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em> (willingness to share resources, following <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bib1" title="" class="ltx_ref">1 (1)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx50" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ils et al. (2021)</a></cite>), <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em> (restriction or exclusion of groups), <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed</em> (both supportive and opposing elements), and <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">none</em> (neutral or unrelated content). Instances labeled as solidarity or anti-solidarity are further assigned one of four frame types adapted from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen &amp; Verheyen (2022)</a></cite>: <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based</em> (shared identity or exclusion), <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate</em> (protection or dismissal of marginalized groups), <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exchange-based</em> (valuing contributions or claiming imbalance in contributions), and <em id="S2.SS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empathic</em> (respect for or rejection of social diversity).</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We extend the evaluation to a newly annotated set of 756 instances for Migrant with more historical coverage (before 1950). <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For this set, annotations were conducted by 4 annotators following the same procedure as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al. (2024)</a></cite> </span>Statistics on the human-annotated dataset are presented in Fig. <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Data Annotation ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>. <span id="S2.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Overall, the label distribution differs notably by target group: woman-related instances are predominantly labeled as solidarity (74.1%), while migrant-related instances show a more diverse pattern, with lower solidarity (51.6%) and a higher share of anti-solidarity (21.3%).</span></p>
</div>
<figure id="S2.F3" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/HumanAnnotatedLabelDistribution.svg" id="S2.F3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:457/181;" width="457" height="181"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S2.F3.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 3</span>: </span><span id="S2.F3.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of instances in the human annotated dataset across time and target groups. <em id="S2.F3.6.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Total</em> indicates number of all annotated samples for the given target group, including the <em id="S2.F3.6.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">None</em> label. See Table <a href="#A3.T2" title="Table 2 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a> for the actual numbers of instances in the human annotated dataset.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">2.3 </span>Models and Experiments</h3>

<div id="S2.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We evaluate a diverse suite of models, ranging from smaller supervised models (BERT, fine-tuned SBERT) to proprietary LLMs (GPT-4) and recent <span id="S2.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">open-weight</span> instruction-tuned models. The final list includes:</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S2.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">smaller pre-trained LMs like BERT and SBERT;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">closed-source LLMs like <span id="S2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4-1106-preview</span>;</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(iii)</span> 
<div id="S2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">open-weight LLMs such as <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-It</span>, <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-It</span> (and fine-tuned <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-70B-It</span>),
<span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-2-9b-It</span>, <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-2-27b-It</span>, and <span id="S2.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mistral-2-Large</span>.</p>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Data and Metrics</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We adopt the 70/15/15 train/dev/test split strategy as in <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al. (2024)</a></cite>, generating three random splits of approximately 2,000 training, 400 development, and 430 test instances for each target group. These are used to train and evaluate supervised models. For inference-only experiments, we use only the test sets: Test 1 (original) and Test 2 (newly added, 756 instances), the latter offering broader historical coverage (1860s–1950s). We report Macro F1 to account for class imbalance, along with per-class F1 scores for both high-level and fine-grained classification tasks.<span id="footnote2" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">2</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">2</span>
                
                
                
              <span id="footnote2.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">High-level classification refers to the four stance categories: solidarity, anti-solidarity, mixed, and none. Fine-grained classification includes ten labels: the four subtypes of solidarity, the four subtypes of anti-solidarity, plus mixed and none.</span></span></span></span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Models</h5>

<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We use manually designed prompts for each target group.
<span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Each prompt consists of a classification instruction concatenated with the parliamentary instance, i.e., the keyword sentence plus its three-sentence left/right context as defined in <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Data ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>. Note that, although speaker and party metadata are available in the corpus, this information is removed from the model input. The classification instruction includes</span> (i) chain-of-thought reasoning in zero- and few-shot settings (via <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">think step by step</span>); (ii) label definitions; and (iii) a two-step classification format: high-level first, followed by subtype for solidarity or anti-solidarity prediction.<span id="footnote3" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">3</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">3</span>
                
                
                
              For GPT-4, both steps are combined in a single prompt (see Figure <a href="#A3.F21" title="Figure 21 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">21</span></a> for <em id="footnote3.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Migrant</em> and Figure <a href="#A3.F22" title="Figure 22 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">22</span></a> in the Appendix for <em id="footnote3.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Woman</em>). For open-weight models, we instead use two prompts: one for high-level classification and, conditional on that output, a second for subtype selection (see Figure <a href="#A3.F24" title="Figure 24 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">24</span></a> for <em id="footnote3.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Migrant</em> and Figure <a href="#A3.F27" title="Figure 27 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">27</span></a> in the Appendix for <em id="footnote3.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Woman</em>).</span></span></span> In few-shot mode, prompts include one representative example per label.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">We apply these prompts to closed-source models like <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">gpt-4-1106-preview</span>, and to open-weight LLMs including <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-Instruct</span>, <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-2-9b-it</span>, and <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-2-27b-it</span> (full precision) in inference-only setting. Larger models (<span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-Instruct</span> and <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mistral-Large-Instruct-2407</span>) are run with <math id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="Q4_{0}" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo lspace="0em" rspace="0em">​</mo><msub><mn>4</mn><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">Q4_{0}</annotation></semantics></math> quantization. Additionally, we fine-tune <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-It</span> and <span id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-70B-It</span> using a three-model pipeline (high-level and two subtype classifiers), applying instruction-style fine-tuning with true labels paired with GPT-4-generated explanations as training targets.</p>
</div>
<div id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S2.SS3.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">We use BERT and SBERT as supervised baselines. Full model configurations, fine-tuning setups, and inference details for all models used in our experiments are provided in <a href="#A3" title="Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">C</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S3" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">3 </span>Results</h2>

<div id="S3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we evaluate models against a human-annotated dataset to determine reliability for social scientific analysis of solidarity frames in parliamentary texts. Our aim is twofold: (i) to identify a model whose predictions approximate human-level annotation quality across both high-level and fine-grained stance categories, and (ii) to ensure the selected model is practically feasible to deploy at scale. <span id="S3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Specifically, we ask whether recent open-weight models can match the performance of GPT-4, which was previously found to be highly reliable (though costly) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, through techniques such as few-shot prompting or lightweight fine-tuning.</span></p>
</div>
<section id="S3.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.1 </span>Model Performance Overview</h3>

<div id="S3.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Table <a href="#S3.T1" title="Table 1 ‣ 3.1 Model Performance Overview ‣ 3 Results ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">1</span></a> reports macro F1 scores for each model across both inference-based and fine-tuned approaches. We benchmark these results against a human upper bound, estimated using a leave-one-out (LOO) procedure,<span id="footnote4" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">4</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">4</span>
              
              
              
            For each annotator, we exclude their labels, compute a consensus label from the remaining annotators, and calculate the macro F1 score between the excluded annotator and the consensus. We then average these scores across all annotators.</span></span></span> and report performance for both high-level and fine-grained classification tasks, with the latter shown in parentheses. Our observations are structured around two dimensions: (i) overall model performance, (ii) task and label-related challenges.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.T1" class="ltx_table">
