<td id="S3.T1.3.13.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.13.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.25 (0.55)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.13.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.13.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.33 (0.48)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.13.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.13.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.26 (0.46)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.14" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.14.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.14.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Gemma-2-9B</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.14.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.14.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Zero-shot</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.14.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.14.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.22 (0.45)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.14.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.14.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.24 (0.44)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.14.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.14.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.22 (0.46)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.15" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.15.1" class="ltx_td" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.15.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.15.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Few-shot</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.15.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.15.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.23 (0.44)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.15.4" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.15.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.31 (0.54)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.15.5" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.15.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.24 (0.46)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.16" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.16.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.3.16.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">BERT (2000 train instances)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.16.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.16.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.13 (0.26)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.16.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.16.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.24 (0.46)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.16.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_t" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.16.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.23 (0.45)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.17" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.17.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.3.17.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">SBERT (2000 train instances)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.17.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.17.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.15 (0.39)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.17.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.17.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.23 (0.43)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.17.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.17.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.18 (0.36)</span></td></tr>
<tr id="S3.T1.3.18" class="ltx_tr">
<td id="S3.T1.3.18.1" class="ltx_td ltx_align_left ltx_border_bb ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;" colspan="2"><span id="S3.T1.3.18.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">Human upper bound</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.18.2" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.18.2.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.34 (0.62)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.18.3" class="ltx_td ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.18.3.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.48 (0.75)</span></td>
<td id="S3.T1.3.18.4" class="ltx_td ltx_nopad_r ltx_align_center ltx_border_bb ltx_border_tt" style="padding-top:0.85pt;padding-bottom:0.85pt;"><span id="S3.T1.3.18.4.1" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:80%;">0.35 (0.57)</span></td></tr>
</table>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering" style="font-size:80%;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_table"><span id="S3.T1.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">Table 1</span>: </span><span id="S3.T1.11" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:113%;">
Comparative performance (macro F1) of models on high-level (in parentheses) and fine-grained tasks. <span id="S3.T1.11.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Migrant (T1)”</span> uses the original benchmark; <span id="S3.T1.11.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“Migrant (T2)”</span> is newly introduced in this paper. Green cells indicate the best-performing score for each target group, based on a model’s best setting (e.g., zero-shot or few-shot); yellow cells indicate the best-performing score from a different model (i.e., the second-best model overall).
</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ol id="S3.I1" class="ltx_enumerate ltx_leftmargin_flush">
<li id="S3.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(i)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Overall model performance.</span></p>
<ul id="S3.I1.i1.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4 achieves the highest scores, but Llama-3.3-70B is a strong alternative.</span>
Across all target groups (<span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Woman</span> and both <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> test sets), <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">GPT-4 (zero-shot)</span> consistently shows the top performance, coming closest to the human upper bound in both high-level and fine-grained classification. <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i1.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.3-70B</span> is the best-performing open model, showing meaningful gains from few-shot prompting (in particular in the high-level classification).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Model size correlates with performance, but not perfectly.</span>
Larger models generally outperform smaller ones on both high-level and fine-grained tasks (see Figure <a href="#S3.F4" title="Figure 4 ‣ 2nd item ‣ item (i) ‣ 3.1 Model Performance Overview ‣ 3 Results ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> for the relationship between model size and performance). Mid-sized models follow, and smaller models perform worst. However, this relationship is not strictly linear: for example, <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama3-8B-it</span> underperforms relative to smaller baselines like <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">BERT</span> and the similarly sized <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-9B-it</span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F4" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/SizePlotHighlevelFinegrainedBestPerformingMigrant.svg" id="S3.F4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/181;" width="476" height="181"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 4</span>: </span><span id="S3.F4.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Model size vs. performance on high-level and fine-grained classification tasks in <span id="S3.F4.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant (T1)</span>. Each model is plotted with its best-performing configuration (zero- or few-shot). The x-axis is logarithmic, so spatial distances do not represent linear differences in parameter count.</span></figcaption>
</figure></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Examples help mid-sized models more than small or very large ones.</span>
Few-shot prompting improves performance primarily for mid-sized models such as <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-9B-it</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Gemma-27B-it</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Mistral-2-Large</span>, and <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-it</span>. In contrast, smaller models like <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-it</span> show no benefit, and <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">GPT-4</span> sees minimal gains—likely because its zero-shot performance is already near-optimal. Fine-tuned variants (e.g., <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3.3-70B-
<br class="ltx_break">it</span>, <span id="S3.I1.i1.I1.i3.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_typewriter">Llama-3-8B-it</span>) often show no improvement over inference-only models.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">(ii)</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Task and label-related challenges.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S3.I1.i2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<ul id="S3.I1.i2.I1" class="ltx_itemize">
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">All models perform worse on the fine-grained task</span>, underscoring its complexity: unlike the more intuitive high-level categories (e.g., solidarity, anti-solidarity), it requires alignment with annotation guidelines grounded in social theory, where even human annotators show limited agreement (reaching macro F1 score 0.34 for Woman and 0.48 for Migrant).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">At the high level, solidarity is the easiest to detect</span> (see Figure <a href="#S3.F5" title="Figure 5 ‣ 3rd item ‣ item (ii) ‣ 3.1 Model Performance Overview ‣ 3 Results ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a>; full scores in Table <a href="#A3.T3" title="Table 3 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> in the Appendix), especially in the <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Woman</span> category, where even smaller models like BERT (0.77) and Gemma-9B (0.78) perform strongly. <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Anti-solidarity and mixed stance are the most challenging categories,</span> with GPT-4 achieving the highest high-level anti-solidarity scores (0.64 for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span>, 0.65 for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Woman</span>), but mid-size models (e.g., Mistral, Llama-3.3-70B, Gemma-27B, Gemma-9B) show moderate performance (with scores ranging from 0.52 to 0.54). Only GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B reliably handle mixed stances (e.g., 0.38–0.43 for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span>, 0.30–0.37 for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Woman</span>).</p>
</div></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">On the fine-grained level, GPT-4, Llama-3.3-70B, and Gemma models offer the most balanced coverage across subtypes.</span> GPT-4 stands out on exchange-based solidarity (0.56), group- and exchange-based anti-solidarity for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> (0.47 and 0.46), and on empathic and compassionate anti-solidarity for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Woman</span> (0.67 and 0.50). Among open models, Llama-3.3-70B performs particularly well on compassionate solidarity (0.51), and shows strong results on group- and exchange-based anti-solidarity (0.42 and 0.35). Interestingly, the Gemma models are able to distinguish the rare compassionate anti-solidarity subtype for <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i3.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> with some success. Most other models struggle with fine-grained anti-solidarity, with scores close to zero.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F5" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/F1-Scores_Dual.svg" id="S3.F5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/168;" width="476" height="168"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 5</span>: </span><span id="S3.F5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">F1 scores for high-level labels based on the best-performing configuration for each model. For the Migrant group, the two test sets (Test 1 and Test 2) are combined.</span></figcaption>
</figure></li>
<li id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4" class="ltx_item" style="list-style-type:none;"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item">•</span> 
<div id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Performance drops on historical data.</span> All models perform worse on the historical <span id="S3.I1.i2.I1.i4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant (T2)</span> set, including the strongest ones. This reflects a broader pattern: model performance tends to decline on older data, as shown by macro F1 scores over time (see Figure <a href="#A3.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a>)—so does agreement among human annotators (Cohen’s Kappa). However, while human trends are smooth and monotonic, model trajectories are less stable. Stronger models such as GPT-4, Mistral-2-Large, Llama-3.3-70B, and BERT show a general upward trend but exhibit clear dips in the 1930s and 1960s. In contrast, smaller models (e.g., Llama-3-8B, SBERT, Gemma-9B) show little to no temporal coherence.</p>
</div></li>
</ul>
</div></li>
</ol>
</div>
<div id="S3.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">In summary, GPT-4 remains the strongest model overall. While performance generally scales with model size, mid-sized models can approximate GPT-4 when given examples. Among open-weight alternatives, Llama-3.3-70B emerges as the most viable option, particularly for the <span id="S3.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> category, delivering competitive results in both high-level and fine-grained classification, while offering a more cost-effective solution. We therefore adopt it for the subsequent social scientific analyses. However, since it still lags behind GPT-4 in capturing certain fine-grained subtypes, we further validate its reliability by comparing the temporal patterns produced by both models in <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Trends with GPT-4 vs with Llama-3.3-70B ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S3.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">3.2 </span>Error Analysis</h3>

<div id="S3.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this subsection, we examine the errors made by the two strongest models in our experiments, GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B, as determined by macro F1 scores. To identify where these models struggle, we compare their confusion matrices on the full <span id="S3.SS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> test set (Figure <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2 Error Analysis ‣ 3 Results ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> for high-level; see Figure <a href="#A3.F17" title="Figure 17 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">17</span></a> in the Appendix for confusion matrices for all models included in the study and Figure <a href="#A3.F18" title="Figure 18 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">18</span></a> in the Appendix for fine-grained label confusion matrices) with a human reference, constructed by averaging confusion matrices from four leave-one-out (LOO) comparisons among annotators.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S3.F6" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/CM_highlevel_HumansLOO_top.svg" id="S3.F6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/144;" width="476" height="144"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S3.F6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 6</span>: </span><span id="S3.F6.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">
High-level confusion matrices showing the best-performing configuration for each model on the two migrant test sets (Test 1 and Test 2), combined. <em id="S3.F6.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Human Annotation</em> scores are computed by averaging the confusion matrices from four LOO comparisons, where each annotator is compared to the consensus of the others. </span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S3.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S3.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Both models exhibit notable differences from human annotators. In particular, both models frequently annotate <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">true Solidarity instances</span> as None (108 instances for GPT-4, 128 for Llama) and <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">true Anti-solidarity instances</span> as None (59 for GPT-4, 60 for Llama), especially for common subtypes such as group-based and compassionate. These errors are far less frequent in human annotations (32 and 12, respectively), suggesting that models may more often underestimate both solidarity and anti-solidarity. <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Llama additionally shows a tendency to misclassify true Solidarity as Anti-solidarity (30 cases vs. 11 for GPT-4 and 13 for human annotators) and, to a lesser extent, true Anti-solidarity as Solidarity (18 vs. 4 for GPT-4 and 10 for humans).</span> Some of these misclassifications arise when a speaker cites an anti-solidarity statement by another party, which Llama occasionally misinterprets as anti-solidarity <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Examples 9 and 11 in Table <a href="#A3.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> in the Appendix; these are discussed in more detail in Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</span> However, manual inspection indicates that <span id="S3.SS2.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">such cases are rare.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">4 </span>Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants</h2>

<div id="S4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this section, we apply the best-performing open-weight model identified in <a href="#S3" title="3 Results ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a>, Llama-3.3-70B, to the full <span id="S4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> dataset of 63k instances to analyze trends across time.
<span id="S4.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">We focus on the <span id="S4.p1.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> dataset in particular because migration has long been one of the most contested issues in German politics, consistently framed through <span id="S4.p1.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“crisis narratives”</span> that have shaped national identity and migration governance across decades <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fröhlich, 2025</a>)</cite>. This makes it a compelling case for evaluating whether model-based annotations align with historical and political science literature. It is also reflected in our human-annotated dataset, where migrant-related instances display a more diverse label distribution, including substantially more anti-solidarity, compared to the predominantly solidarity-oriented discourse on women.</span>
More specifically, <a href="#S4.SS1" title="4.1 Trends with GPT-4 vs with Llama-3.3-70B ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.1</span></a> compares the long-term (anti-)solidarity trends across human annotators and LLMs and thereby further validates the results of our models. In doing so, we also note two periods with notable shifts in solidarity, namely the post-war period and the recent past, which we cover in more detail in Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a> and Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F7" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/GPT_Highlevel_1867-2022.svg" id="S4.F7.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/302;" width="476" height="302"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GPT-4</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F7.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/Llama3_Highlevel_1867-2022.svg" id="S4.F7.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/302;" width="476" height="302"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F7.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Llama-3.3-70B</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F7.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 7</span>: </span><span id="S4.F7.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Solidarity trends for <span id="S4.F7.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> predicted by GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B. <span id="S4.F7.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Values are normalized within each decade; percentages may not total 100% because instances classified as <span id="S4.F7.7.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“None”</span> are not shown (though included in the calculation).</span> The period 1933–1949 <span id="S4.F7.7.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(grey shaded area)</span> is excluded due to limited data availability during and immediately after the Nazi dictatorship.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<section id="S4.SS1" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.1 </span>Trends with GPT-4 vs with Llama-3.3-70B</h3>

<figure id="S4.F8" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/Finegrained_1867-2022_GPT.svg" id="S4.F8.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/194;" width="476" height="194"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F8.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">GPT-4</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F8.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/Finegrained_1867-2022_Llama3.svg" id="S4.F8.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/194;" width="476" height="194"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F8.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Llama-3.3-70B</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F8.6" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 8</span>: </span><span id="S4.F8.7" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(Anti-)solidarity frames trends for <span id="S4.F8.7.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Migrant</span> predicted by GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B. <span id="S4.F8.7.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Percentages are normalized within each decade across all eight subtypes; thus, the curves in both panels together sum to 100% per decade.</span> The period from 1933 to 1949 <span id="S4.F8.7.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(grey shaded area)</span> is excluded due to limited data availability during and immediately after the NS dictatorship.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figures <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a> and <a href="#S4.F8" title="Figure 8 ‣ 4.1 Trends with GPT-4 vs with Llama-3.3-70B ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">8</span></a> show the development of (anti-)solidarity for GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B on a high and fine-grained level across 155 years. The GPT-4 reference data is taken from <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx57" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kostikova et al. (2024)</a></cite>, based on a subset of 18.3k migrant-related instances (from an original 58k-instance dataset; see <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Data ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>) spanning 1867–2022. While Llama-3.3-70B is applied to the full dataset (expanded to 63k instances through 2025) for the main analysis, we restrict it to the same 18.3k-instance subset for this comparison.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">To validate the annotations of the models, we first compare them to each other. Comparing the trends between GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B, we observe a high correlation of 0.96 on the high level and between 0.77 and 0.95 across the more frequent fine-grained subtypes (see Table <a href="#A3.T4" title="Table 4 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4</span></a> in the Appendix). This indicates that the models show a high degree of agreement in temporal trends.<span id="footnote5" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">5</sup>
              <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">5</span>
              
              
              
            <span id="footnote5.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Additionally, Llama-3.3-70B shows near-identical trends between the 18.3k subset and the full 63k dataset (e.g., <math id="footnote5.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.996 for solidarity and anti-solidarity, and <math id="footnote5.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\sim" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mo style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">∼</mo><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\sim</annotation></semantics></math>0.97–0.98 for subtypes).</span></span></span></span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For reference, Figure <a href="#S2.F3" title="Figure 3 ‣ 2.2 Data Annotation ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">3</span></a> shows the high-level trends across time for human annotations (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m1" class="ltx_Math" alttext="N=2,084" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2,084</mn></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">N=2,084</annotation></semantics></math>). We observe similar relative distributions of (anti-)solidarity on a high level. <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Direct trend correlations between Llama-3.3-70B and human annotations are also substantial (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.84 for anti-solidarity, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m3" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.76 for solidarity, <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m4" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\rho" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;" mathcolor="#000000">ρ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\rho</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.47 for mixed).</span> In terms of Cohen’s <math id="S4.SS1.p3.m5" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> <span id="S4.SS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(computed on the 2,084 human annotated instances)</span>, GPT-4 slightly outperforms Llama-3.3-70B (<math id="S4.SS1.p3.m6" class="ltx_Math" alttext="\kappa" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mi>κ</mi><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">\kappa</annotation></semantics></math> = 0.57 vs. 0.50 at the high level; 0.38 vs. 0.36 for subtypes), but differences are modest. Overall, GPT-4 and Llama-3.3-70B appear to produce similar results that agree to a moderate level with human annotations. Given that it is an open weight model that we can run on our own computing hardware at low further cost, we utilize Llama-3.3-70B for the subsequent analysis.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.1 </span>Trend Stability Across Keywords.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Before we move on to interpreting the observed trends derived from Llama-3.3-70B predictions, we verify that they are not artifacts of our keyword-based data collection (see <a href="#S2.SS1" title="2.1 Data ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2.1</span></a>). As Figures <a href="#A3.F19" title="Figure 19 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">19</span></a> and <a href="#A3.F20" title="Figure 20 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">20</span></a> in the Appendix show, individual keywords vary considerably in their temporal distribution and in the balance of solidarity vs. anti-solidarity. To rule out the possibility that our long-term trends merely reflect such keyword effects, we perform two robustness tests.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">First, we conduct a stability test by randomly sampling 200 subsets of at least 5 keywords each and comparing the resulting high-level trends using Pearson’s correlation coefficient. Across all 19,900 pairwise comparisons, the trends are highly consistent (average correlation of 0.92 for solidarity, 0.81 for anti-solidarity, and 0.94 for mixed; with 25% quantiles of 0.90, 0.70, and 0.92, respectively).<span id="footnote6" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">6</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote6.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">6</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote6.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For more detail on keywords and the stability test, see </span><a href="#A1" title="Appendix A List of Keywords ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">A</span></a><span id="footnote6.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">.</span></span></span></span></span> <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Second, we restrict the analysis to the two most frequent and temporally widespread keywords, <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ausländer</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: foreigners) and <span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Flüchtlinge</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS1.p2.1.2.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: refugees), which occur in every decade (19.7k and 12k mentions, respectively). Together, the resulting plots (Figure <a href="#A3.F23" title="Figure 23 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">23</span></a> in the Appendix) and the stability test results confirm that overall trends in solidarity and anti-solidarity are preserved both under this restricted vocabulary and across different keyword selections.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS1.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.1.2 </span>Notable Trends in 1867-2022.</h4>

<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Inspecting the trends in Figure <a href="#S4.F7" title="Figure 7 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">7</span></a>, we observe four periods with notable shifts in solidarity: Between 1890-1920, in the 1930s, in the post-war period, and in the recent past after 2010.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Regarding the 1890-1920 period, we observe some disagreement between the models: GPT-4 shows an increase in solidarity between 1900 and 1920, while Llama-3.3-70B does not. We also note that the agreement between human annotators and between models and annotators is lower for historic data, such that we do not consider this trend to be sufficiently reliable for further analysis.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The 1930s and 1940s data points</span> are outliers, as the parliament was swiftly sidelined by the Nazi regime and the only 109 available samples from 1933 to 1949 mostly concern inter-state relations (mentioning, e.g., <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“foreign creditors”</span>) that fall into the <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“None”</span> category. Hence, we exclude these data points from further analysis, as well.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p">For the two remaining periods, 1949–1957 and 2009–2025, we take a more detailed look. <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">In the postwar years (Section <a href="#S4.SS2" title="4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.2</span></a>), parliamentary discourse reveals high levels of solidarity and little anti-solidarity toward migrants, despite the severe economic and infrastructural strain of the time <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx14" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braun &amp; Kvasnicka, 2014</a>)</cite>. This raises our first guiding question: <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Why is there so much solidarity (and so little anti-solidarity), and what was it based on?</em> In contrast, the recent period (Section <a href="#S4.SS3" title="4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">4.3</span></a>) shows a clear rise in anti-solidarity and a decline in solidarity, beginning in the early 2010s. These shifts likely reflect the political impact of recent refugee movements and the growing influence of anti-immigration views, such as those promoted by the far-right party <span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Alternative für Deutschland (AfD)</span> (<span id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Alternative for Germany), which has gained substantial electoral support in Germany since its founding in 2013.
This leads to our second question: <em id="S4.SS1.SSS2.p4.1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">What are the drivers of anti-solidarity in the contemporary period?</em></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.2 </span>Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957</h3>

<div id="S4.SS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">The Second World War set off the largest wave of forced migrations in European history, with tens of millions of people, both non-Germans and ethnic Germans, displaced or resettled across Central and Eastern Europe <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx89" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schulze, 2017</a>)</cite>. The populations displaced by the war were highly heterogeneous, including refugees, displaced persons (DPs), forced laborers, and—forming the largest group of expellees after 1945 in both Europe as a whole and Germany—ethnic Germans. For those who settled in postwar Germany, integration posed significant political and societal challenges, shaped by regional disparities, economic hardship, and tensions with the local population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx21" title="" class="ltx_ref">Chevalier et al., 2024</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">This subsection focuses on the early postwar period (1949–1957), when the Federal Republic of Germany institutionalized solidarity as part of nation-building. This period is analytically important because solidarity was no longer merely a moral gesture, but a tool of governance, embedded in legal frameworks like the <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lastenausgleich (LAG)</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Equalization of Burdens),
which redistributed resources between the war-damaged and the less affected to promote social cohesion and democratic legitimacy <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kittel, 2020</a>)</cite>. <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Crucially, such measures applied specifically to <span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Volkszugehörige</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.p2.1.3.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: ethnic Germans), excluding other migrant groups from the benefits provided by these laws.</span> However, since solidarity in this period was neither static nor uniform, shaped by diverse migrant groups, competing political ideologies, and contested notions of deservingness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx95" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ther, 1996</a>)</cite>, we draw on Thijssen’s typology of solidarity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen, 2012</a>)</cite> to guide our analysis. By distinguishing between identity-, need-, and contribution-based forms, this framework allows us to examine how solidarity was constructed and sustained amid institutional strain.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.1 </span>Overall solidarity distribution</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Throughout the 1949–1957 period, solidarity dominated parliamentary discourse on postwar population movements. As shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F9" title="Figure 9 ‣ 4.2.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">9</span></a>,<span id="footnote7" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">7</sup>
                <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note">7</span>
                
                
                
              We use raw counts as they provide a more interpretable view of stance distribution during this period, given the overwhelming dominance of solidarity and the low frequency of categories like anti-solidarity.</span></span></span> mentions of solidarity consistently outpaced both anti-solidarity and mixed statements, at times reaching ten times their frequency.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F9" class="ltx_figure"><img src="2509.07274v1/1949-1957_Highlevel.png" id="S4.F9.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:405/286;" width="405" height="286" alt="Refer to caption">
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F9.9" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 9</span>: </span><span id="S4.F9.10" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of (Anti-)solidarity mentions in Bundestag debates, 1949–1957 (raw counts). LAG refers to the <span id="S4.F9.10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Lastenausgleichsgesetz</span> (<span id="S4.F9.10.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Equalisation of Burdens Law); BVFG to the <span id="S4.F9.10.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bundesvertriebenengesetz</span> (<span id="S4.F9.10.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Federal Expellees Act); <span id="S4.F9.10.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">HAuslG to the Gesetz über die Rechtsstellung heimatloser Ausländer im Bundesgebiet</span> (<span id="S4.F9.10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Law on the Legal Status of Stateless Foreigners in the Federal Territory).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Mentions of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em> closely track key legislative moments, particularly in the debates leading up to the passage of major laws. A major spike in March 1950 aligns with early discussions on the integration of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">expellees</span> and Article 131, which addressed the rehabilitation of former public servants <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">of the Nazi regime</span>, many of them displaced. A second cluster from January to May 1952 reflects debates on the LAG, preceding its adoption in May <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see an excerpt from such a debate in Example 1 in Table <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> in the Appendix).</span> Another sharp rise in February–March 1953 corresponds to readings of the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bundesvertriebenengesetz (BVFG)</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Federal Expellee <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Law</span>), which defined the legal status of expellees and refugees and codified rights and integration measures, <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.8" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">see Example 2 for such a debate.</span> Later peaks in September 1954 and June 1955 reflect continued debate over LAG implementation and budget issues. Smaller but sustained increases in 1956 relate to new support measures for <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Sowjetzonenflüchtlinge</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: Soviet-zone refugees) and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aussiedler</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p2.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: resettlers).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F10" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/1949-1957_Finegrained_WithInset.svg" id="S4.F10.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/197;" width="476" height="197"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F10.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 10</span>: </span><span id="S4.F10.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of <span id="S4.F10.5.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">(Anti-)solidarity</span> frame subtypes from 1949 to 1957 (raw counts). An inset plot shows the evolving composition of solidarity subtypes, normalized within each 6-month period. Values represent the proportional share of each subtype among all solidarity mentions during that period.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The prominence of solidarity in this period is unsurprising. Parliamentary debates used it not only to justify compensation, but also to navigate postwar fragmentation and competing claims to victimhood and belonging <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hughes, 1999</a>)</cite>. This multifaceted nature of solidarity is evident in the distribution of all four subtypes across key legislative moments, as shown in Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.2.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>. <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Group-based</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate</em> frames were especially prominent, reflecting an emphasis on both shared national belonging and moral obligation toward displaced populations (see, e.g., Example 1 in Table <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, where solidarity is tied to the right to the homeland and framed as a legal entitlement). More striking is the shift in the balance between these two subtypes over time. As shown in the inset of Figure <a href="#S4.F10" title="Figure 10 ‣ 4.2.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em> dominated in the early 1950s but declined after mid-1953, while <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate</em> steadily increased. This suggests a rhetorical transition: once legal belonging was formalized, most notably <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">with the LAG in 1952</span> and BVFG in 1953, solidarity no longer needed to be framed primarily in terms of shared identity. The shift may also reflect demographic change within the displaced populations, as newer arrivals like <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sowjetzonenflüchtlinge</em> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.7" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">from the GDR</span> and <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Aussiedler</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: resettlers) <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">from the Eastern Bloc countries</span> were less easily framed as co-nationals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Böke, 1996</a>)</cite>. This trend aligns with Thijssen’s reading of Durkheim: as group-based solidarity extends to more diverse groups, it gives way to need-based appeals <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx96" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen, 2012</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 3 in Table <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a> in the Appendix)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">These patterns show that solidarity was used in parliamentary debates to manage the challenges of postwar reconstruction, as reflected in key legislative reforms. <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">While</span> the claimant groups <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">of the broadest measure LAG</span> were highly diverse <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(bombed out, evacuated, expelled, financially damaged),
their</span> eligibility for compensation under the law was based on <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Volkszugehörigkeit</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: German ethnicity), excluding many from formal recognition <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kittel, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx72" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nachum, 2013</a>)</cite>. <span id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The concept of Volksdeutsche was a fluid and constructed Nazi category whose ambiguous definition nonetheless had concrete and deadly implications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx9" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bergen, 2005</a>)</cite>.</span> We therefore examine how <em id="S4.SS2.SSS1.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">(anti-)solidarity</em> subtypes were expressed across key migrant-related terms in parliamentary discourse in that period.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.2 </span>Solidarity Frames Across Keywords in 1949-1957</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">This analysis includes only migrant-related keywords that appear at least 200 times in combination with a <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em>, <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em> subtype or <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">mixed</em> stance. Keywords dominated by <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">none</em> or with low total counts are excluded (e.g., <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Asylsuchende</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: asylum seekers), <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Einwanderer</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: immigrants), <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Emigranten</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p1.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: emigrants), etc.); see Table <a href="#A3.T5" title="Table 5 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">5</span></a> for full distributions.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F11" title="Figure 11 ‣ 4.2.2 Solidarity Frames Across Keywords in 1949-1957 ‣ 4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">11</span></a> shows the distribution of (anti-)solidarity frames across migrant-related keywords in 1949–1957. Across all groups, solidarity was far more common than anti-solidarity, with group-based and compassionate frames dominating. However, the distribution of subtypes varies between groups.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Vertriebene<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (</span>en.<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">: expellees)</span></span> are most often associated with <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em>, accounting for around half of all expressions with a stance, while also receiving a substantial share of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate solidarity</em> (nearly 30%). This reflects their dual position: as key representatives of German victimhood (alongside prisoners of war) during and after the <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">World War II</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx70" title="" class="ltx_ref">Moeller, 2001</a>)</cite>, they were both integrated into West Germany’s nation-building narrative and portrayed as victims of Allied or foreign injustice. Still, around 5–6% of stance expressions toward <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Heimatvertriebene</em> fall into the <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em> category, particularly <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based</em> (solidarity limited to <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.8" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“us”</span>, the in-group). These expressed concerns that expellees strained local infrastructures and were unfairly privileged over equally suffering <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Einheimische</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: locals), mirroring broader public resentment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx56" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kossert, 2008</a>)</cite>. Perceptions of injustice were heightened by the uneven distribution of expellees across West German states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx15" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braun &amp; Mahmoud, 2014</a>)</cite>, particularly in regions where their prominent presence in public institutions and politics sparked fears of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Überfremdung</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: overforeignization) and contributed to a sense of unequal burden-sharing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx63" title="" class="ltx_ref">Levy &amp; Sznaider, 2005</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p3.1.13" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">see Example 4 in Table <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a></span>.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F11" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/KeywordsFinegrained.svg" id="S4.F11.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/155;" width="476" height="155"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F11.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 11</span>: </span><span id="S4.F11.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Relative distribution of (Anti-)Solidarity subtypes across selected migrant-related keywords, 1949–1957. Bars represent the proportion of each subtype within each keyword group, thus all subtypes for a given group sum to 100%.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Flüchtlinge<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (</span>en.<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">: refugees), </span>Sowjetzonenflüchtlinge<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (</span>en.<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">: Soviet-zone refugees), and </span>Aussiedler<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (</span>en.<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">: resettlers)</span></span> are primarily framed through <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate solidarity</em> (45–52%), followed by <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em> (25–30%). <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The label <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.4.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Flüchtlinge</em> initially functioned as a broad catch-all category in the late 1940s <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nachum &amp; Schaefer, 2018</a>)</cite>.</span> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">With the 1953 BVFG, it was more clearly differentiated into <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vertriebene</em>, <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sowjetzonenflüchtlinge</em>, and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Aussiedler</em>. Yet their Germanness was not valued equally: while <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vertriebene</em> were framed as victims of expulsion and central to West German nation-building, the label <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.5.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Flüchtlinge</em> retained post-war connotations of marginality and foreignness <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nachum &amp; Schaefer, 2018</a>)</cite>.</span>
This helps explain a higher share of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.6" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate solidarity</em>, as well as the notable <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.7" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em> (around 10%). <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.8" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Sowjetzonenflüchtlinge</em>, in turn, were primarily framed as Cold War refugees fleeing communist oppression and thus not integrated into the nation building narrative like the <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.9" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vertriebene</em>, prompting more <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.10" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate</em> than <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.11" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx73" title="" class="ltx_ref">Nachum &amp; Schaefer, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx13" title="" class="ltx_ref">Böke, 1996</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p4.1.12" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 5)</span>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold ltx_font_italic">Ausländer<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright"> (</span>en.<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_upright">: foreigners)</span></span> were the main targets of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em>. Not a single coherent group, they were a discursively constructed <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“other”</span>, including DPs, Eastern European border crossers, wartime laborers, and early foreign workers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx92" title="" class="ltx_ref">Smith, 1994</a>)</cite>. Often contrasted with German groups like Vertriebene or Einheimische, they were framed as non-belonging, economically burdensome, and sometimes criminalized <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holian &amp; Wilhelm, 2017</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 6).</span>
<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">However, despite this exclusionary framing, solidarity with Ausländer, especially <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.5.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based</em>, exceeded anti-solidarity. This might be partly due to the category’s fluidity, which included ethnic Germans not yet formally recognized as such <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruhkopf, 2023</a>)</cite> in the early postwar years. Politicians like Lukaschek also linked DPs and expellees through shared narratives of lost <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.5.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Heimat</span> (<span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.5.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: homeland), enabling symbolic inclusion into the national victim community <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruhkopf, 2023</a>)</cite> (Example 7). The second-highest solidarity subtype, <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.5.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exchange-based solidarity</em>, reflects the perceived political utility of <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.5.5" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ausländer</span> in strengthening West Germany’s international legitimacy within the Western alliance <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruhkopf, 2023</a>)</cite> (Example 8). The 1951 Homeless Foreigners Law (HAuslG), passed under Allied pressure, granted DPs civic and social rights largely to meet conditions for sovereignty and improve the country’s international standing <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx44" title="" class="ltx_ref">Holian &amp; Wilhelm, 2017</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx85" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ruhkopf, 2023</a>)</cite>.</span> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">At the same time, instances of genuine solidarity toward non-ethnic German foreigners, particularly in CDU and SPD, statements, show that solidarity with <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p5.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ausländer</span> was not limited to Germanness or foreign policy utility but also reflected broader humanitarian commitments (see Examples 7 and 9).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1" class="ltx_p">In sum, solidarity was expressed differently depending on the migrant group and was shaped by perceived <span id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“deservingness”</span>. <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Vertriebene</em> were largely included as part of the national community, while others, especially <em id="S4.SS2.SSS2.p6.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">Ausländer</em>, were treated as outsiders and received comparatively less support. These differences reflected not only group characteristics but also the ideological strategies of political parties. The next subsection examines how different parties in the early Bundestag framed (anti-)solidarity in their parliamentary speech.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS2.SSS3" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.2.3 </span>Distribution across parties</h4>

<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Figure <a href="#S4.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 4.2.3 Distribution across parties ‣ 4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a> shows the distribution of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em> and <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em> subtypes across parties for the period 1949–1957. While <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p1.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em> was the dominant stance overall, parties varied in how they framed it, driven by ideological and strategic choices. Some parties used solidarity to promote national integration, while others employed it to defend regional concerns. Broadly, we divide parties into three categories: mainstream governing parties, expellee advocacy parties, and smaller parties with ideological or regional agendas.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The main parties – governing CDU/CSU and FDP, and opposition SPD –</span> supported the integration of expellees and refugees through legal and institutional measures, primarily expressing <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based</em> or <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">compassionate</em> solidarity (see Figure <a href="#S4.F12" title="Figure 12 ‣ 4.2.3 Distribution across parties ‣ 4.2 Germany after the Second World War, 1949-1957 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">12</span></a>). The CDU/CSU played a leading role in proposing the LAG, which framed solidarity as a national obligation toward fellow Germans, and even included expellee representatives in the party ranks <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx49" title="" class="ltx_ref">Hughes, 1999</a>)</cite>. The SPD also supported the law but voiced concerns about former Nazis and communist agents among the newcomers <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Connor, 2018</a>)</cite>, resulting in occasional anti-solidarity statements. The FDP, while formally aligned with CDU/CSU and SPD on key refugee issues, framed solidarity in more individualistic, <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.4" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">exchange-based</em> terms, opposing large-scale redistribution and favoring market-based solutions consistent with its liberal economic orientation <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Connor, 2018</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p2.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 10 in Table <a href="#A3.T10" title="Table 10 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">10</span></a>, where FDP speakers argued that expellees should build their own homes through self-help and cooperative effort, with state support limited to loans and low-interest credit).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Expellee advocacy parties (GB/BHE and DP)<span id="footnote8" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">8</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote8.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">8</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote8.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gesamtdeutscher Block/Bund der Heimatvertriebenen und Entrechteten (GB/BHE)</span><span id="footnote8.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span><span id="footnote8.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span><span id="footnote8.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: All-German Bloc/League of Expellees and Deprived of Rights) and </span><span id="footnote8.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Deutsche Partei (DP)</span><span id="footnote8.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span><span id="footnote8.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span><span id="footnote8.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: German Party)</span></span></span></span></span> explicitly positioned themselves as political representatives of displaced Germans. <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Following multiple successes in state elections, GB/BHE entered the federal government coalition in 1953, thereby exerting significant pressure on other parties to address the demands and concerns of expellees <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Connor, 2018</a>)</cite>.</span> They framed solidarity primarily in terms of historical injustice and economic entitlement, calling for wealth redistribution, fair pensions, and affordable housing. Their rhetoric relied heavily on <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em>, portraying expellees as full members of the national community who deserved compensation and full integration <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 11).</span> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Eventually, both major parties of the Federal Republic CDU and SPD managed to absorb the demands and concerns of the expellees, which made GB/BHE and DP less successful <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Connor, 2018</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx54" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kittel, 2020</a>)</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Regionalist parties such as the WAV and BP<span id="footnote9" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">9</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote9.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">9</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote9.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Wirtschaftliche Aufbau-Vereinigung</span><span id="footnote9.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span><span id="footnote9.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span><span id="footnote9.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Economic Reconstruction Union) and </span><span id="footnote9.10" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Bayernpartei</span><span id="footnote9.11" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span><span id="footnote9.12" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span><span id="footnote9.13" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Bavarian Party)</span></span></span></span></span> emphasized <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em>, but restricted it to ethnic Germans. The WAV, a short-lived nationalist party, appealed to expellees through anti-communist rhetoric and demands to reclaim eastern territories <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx59" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kranenpohl, 1998</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Example 12).</span> The BP, responding to Bavaria’s disproportionate refugee burden, called for collective responsibility in crisis, fairer redistribution and showed preference for groups like the Sudeten Germans <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx16" title="" class="ltx_ref">Braun &amp; Dwenger, 2020</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 13,where the BP argued that Sudeten and Southeast Germans should be concentrated in Bavaria because they were <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“closest to the Bavarian cultural sphere”</span> and thus easier to integrate).</span> Both parties also expressed the highest levels of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p4.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">anti-solidarity</em>, either by excluding non-German migrants (WAV) <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Connor, 2018</a>)</cite> or opposing specific groups such as Silesians (BP).</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F12" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/1949-1957_Parties.svg" id="S4.F12.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/154;" width="476" height="154"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F12.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 12</span>: </span><span id="S4.F12.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(Anti-)solidarity frames distribution across major parties in the Bundestag from 1949 to 1957, by election period. Percentages are normalized within each party, such that all subtype bars (solidarity and anti-solidarity) together sum to 100% per party. We include only those parties with a sufficient number of stanced instances <math id="S4.F12.m2" class="ltx_Math" alttext="(N&gt;100)" display="inline" intent=":literal"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy="false">(</mo><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&gt;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow><mo stretchy="false">)</mo></mrow><annotation encoding="application/x-tex">(N&gt;100)</annotation></semantics></math>. The full label distribution across all parties is provided in Figure <a href="#A3.T6" title="Table 6 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a> in the Appendix.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The KPD<span id="footnote10" class="ltx_note ltx_role_footnote"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup><span class="ltx_note_outer"><span class="ltx_note_content"><sup class="ltx_note_mark">10</sup>
                  <span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_note"><span id="footnote10.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">10</span></span>
                  
                  
                  
                <span id="footnote10.6" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Kommunistische Partei Deutschlands</span><span id="footnote10.7" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium"> (</span><span id="footnote10.8" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span><span id="footnote10.9" class="ltx_text ltx_font_medium">: Communist Party of Germany)</span></span></span></span></span> framed displaced populations through a lens of socialist integration, emphasizing <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.2" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">group-based solidarity</em> to support their incorporation into the new social order <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx90" title="" class="ltx_ref">Schwartz, 2008</a>)</cite>. This <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.3" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">solidarity</em>, however, was assimilationist rather than inclusive: the party avoided the term <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.4" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Vertriebene</span>, in line with Soviet repression of expellee identity <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx42" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heidemeyer, 2007</a>)</cite>, and this was reflected in its minimal use of <em id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.5" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">empathic solidarity</em>. The KPD referred almost exclusively to <span id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p5.1.6" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“refugees”</span>, advocating land reform, compensation, and integration as part of a broader effort to portray them as victims of fascism and capitalism and to gain political support <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx22" title="" class="ltx_ref">Connor, 2018</a>)</cite> (see Example 14, where the KPD argued that refugee employment could not be solved under capitalism).</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p6" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS2.SSS3.p6.1" class="ltx_p">Some parties active in the early postwar years, such as the KPD, WAV, and BP, did not stay in parliament for long, but they played an important role in shaping political debates of the time. Rather than merely reflecting existing social tensions, as suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx66" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lipset (1967)</a></cite>, these parties actively contributed to defining what solidarity meant and who was considered deserving of support. As suggested by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen &amp; Verheyen (2022)</a></cite>, such framings were shaped by the political and social challenges of the period and reflected broader efforts to maintain or renegotiate social cohesion.</p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3" class="ltx_subsection">
<h3 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsection">4.3 </span>Recent German history 2009-2025</h3>

<div id="S4.SS3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Between 2009-2015, migration into Germany increased from just under 800,000 people per year to over 2 mio. people per year <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gessler &amp; Hunger, 2023</a>)</cite> in the context of the war in Syria. The peak in migration in 2015 has since been associated with the label <span id="S4.SS3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“migration crisis”</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx41" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gessler &amp; Hunger, 2023</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx36" title="" class="ltx_ref">Fröhlich, 2025</a>)</cite>. Since then, the right-wing AfD has made electoral gains and entered the German federal parliament in 2017. Another spike in migration occurred in 2022 due to the Russian invasion of Ukraine <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx33" title="" class="ltx_ref">Federal Office for Migration and Refugees, 2025</a>)</cite>. Alongside these historical events in the last four parliamentary periods (2009-2025), substantial shifts in solidarity occurred.</p>
</div>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS1" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.1 </span>Overall solidarity distribution</h4>

<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F13" title="Figure 13 ‣ 4.3.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13</span></a> shows the (anti-)solidarity trends according to Llama-3.3-70b annotations between 2009-2025. We observe a decrease in solidarity between 2014-2018, a somewhat stable period between 2018-2022, and another decrease in solidarity 2022-2025. The decrease in solidarity is associated with an almost corresponding increase in anti-solidarity In terms of (anti-)solidarity subtypes, we observe a peak in compassionate solidarity in 2014, followed by a sharp decrease. The decrease in solidarity since 2022 corresponds to a decrease across solidarity subtypes, especially in compassionate and exchange-based solidarity. In terms of anti-solidarity, we observe a strong increase in group-based anti-solidarity, accounting for over 50% of all migrant-related statements that either expressed solidarity or anti-solidarity. Exchange-based anti-solidarity exhibited a small increase between 2015-2020 but dropped again with the beginning of the last parliamentary period in 2021.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Overall, the 2020s mark the first time in post-war history that the fraction of anti-solidarity statements exceeds that of solidarity statements. This key finding is not only visible in the data provided by Llama-3.3-70B but also in human annotations (Figure <a href="#S2.F2" title="Figure 2 ‣ 2.1 Data ‣ 2 Methodology ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">2</span></a>). To provide more insight into this finding, we contextualize it with recent Germany history and a deeper look into party distribution.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F13" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F13.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-HighLevel_2009-2025.svg" id="S4.F13.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/302;" width="476" height="302"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F13.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F13.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Solidarity and anti-solidarity distribution</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F13.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_NormalizedAcrossGroups_2009-2025.svg" id="S4.F13.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F13.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F13.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Solidarity and anti-solidarity frames distribution</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F13.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 13</span>: </span><span id="S4.F13.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Fig. <a href="#S4.F13.sf1" title="In Figure 13 ‣ 4.3.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13(a)</span></a> shows the fraction of solidarity, anti-solidarity, and mixed stance towards migrants from 2009 to 2025 (<span id="S4.F13.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">instances classified as <span id="S4.F13.5.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“None”</span> are not shown, though included in the calculation).</span> Fig. <a href="#S4.F13.sf2" title="In Figure 13 ‣ 4.3.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13(b)</span></a> shows the fraction of solidarity (left) and anti-solidarity (right) subtypes according to Llama-3.3-70B, where percentages are normalized within each year across all eight subtypes (thus, the curves in both panels together sum to 100% per year).</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">The spike in compassionate solidarity 2014 coincides with a time of heightened public solidarity with Syrian refugees:
15.4% of German survey respondents indicated solidarity as the most important personal value in 2014, up from 12.3% in 2013 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al., 2023</a>, p. 38)</cite>. Syrian refugees were commonly framed as needing help, motivating calls for a <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“German welcome culture”</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebauer, 2023</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 15 in Table <a href="#A3.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> in the Appendix).</span> Over the course of 2015, a counter-narrative emerged, framing the influx of refugees as overburdening German capacities and as a <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“refugee crisis”</span> <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebauer, 2023</a>)</cite> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 16).</span> During the same period, the party AfD—founded in 2013 with a focus on EU criticism—aligned itself with anti-migrant protests and moved notably right, followed by substantial electoral gains in both state and national elections and the AfD entering the German parliament in 2017 <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frey, 2020</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebauer, 2023</a>)</cite>. Regarding the broader European context, <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al. (2023)</a></cite> diagnose a <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.5" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“solidarity fatigue”</span> across Europe after 2015, driven by an inability to sustain prolonged compassion on the individual level and dwindling resources on the institutional level. The counter-narratives mentioned by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx39" title="" class="ltx_ref">Gebauer (2023)</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx35" title="" class="ltx_ref">Frey (2020)</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al. (2023)</a></cite> match several notions of anti-solidarity according to <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx97" title="" class="ltx_ref">Thijssen &amp; Verheyen (2022)</a></cite>: refugees as an outgroup that stands in competition to the German population (group-based anti-solidarity), refugees as taking away resources and not contributing sufficiently (exchange-based anti-solidarity), and refugees as not deserving of compassion (compassionate anti-solidarity); <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.6" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">see Example 17 where refugees are described as not contributing, straining resources, and threatening the welfare of the <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p3.1.6.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“real”</span> German population.</span> We also note a general shift towards right-wing sentiments in the general German population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zick et al., 2023</a>)</cite>, which may be associated with heightened anti-solidarity.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1" class="ltx_p">More recently, the Russian invasion in Ukraine and the subsequent migration of Ukrainian refugees into Germany triggered another spike in public solidarity, albeit much less prominent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx103" title="" class="ltx_ref">Weber et al., 2024a</a>)</cite>. In our data, solidarity increases slightly in 2022, followed by a continued, rapid decline. <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx12" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bojarskich et al. (2025)</a></cite> identified several factors promoting anti-solidarity towards Ukraine, such as conspiracy mentality and populist voting, but also fears about economic and societal consequences, indicating exchange-based (insufficient contribution) or group-based (competition between groups) anti-solidarity <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(Example 18).</span> <span id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p4.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Additionally, anti-Ukrainian framing in parliamentary debates may have been reinforced by the AfD’s long-standing pro-Russian alignment <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx5" title="" class="ltx_ref">Arzheimer, 2023</a>)</cite>, including opposition to sanctions and calls to end German military aid to Ukraine.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS1.p5.1" class="ltx_p">In contrast to the postwar period, (anti-)solidarity shifts in the German parliament between 2009 and 2025 appear less attached to specific legislative initiatives. Because of Germany’s membership in the European Union, migration policy has become more complex and is negotiated between European institutions and member states <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx40" title="" class="ltx_ref">Geddes et al., 2020</a>)</cite>. Additionally, the AfD integrated anti-migrant talking points across a wide range of legislative topics. As such, we do not look deeper into specific laws passed during this time frame but rather focus on the trends for specific parties.</p>
</div>
<figure id="S4.F14" class="ltx_figure"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/Utterances-Per-Party-Per-Year.svg" id="S4.F14.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:286/161;" width="286" height="161"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F14.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 14</span>: </span><span id="S4.F14.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Raw yearly counts of parliamentary statements by selected political parties, 2009–2025.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
</section>
<section id="S4.SS3.SSS2" class="ltx_subsubsection">
<h4 class="ltx_title ltx_title_subsubsection"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_subsubsection">4.3.2 </span>Distribution across parties</h4>

<figure id="S4.F15" class="ltx_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_figure">
<div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F15.sf1" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_AfD.svg" id="S4.F15.sf1.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.sf1.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(a)</span> </span><span id="S4.F15.sf1.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">AfD</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F15.sf2" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_CDU-CSU.svg" id="S4.F15.sf2.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.sf2.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(b)</span> </span><span id="S4.F15.sf2.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">CDU/CSU</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F15.sf3" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_SPD.svg" id="S4.F15.sf3.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.sf3.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(c)</span> </span><span id="S4.F15.sf3.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">SPD</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F15.sf4" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_FDP.svg" id="S4.F15.sf4.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.sf4.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(d)</span> </span><span id="S4.F15.sf4.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">FDP</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_break"></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F15.sf5" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_Linke.svg" id="S4.F15.sf5.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.sf5.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(e)</span> </span><span id="S4.F15.sf5.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Die Linke</span></figcaption>
</figure></div><div class="ltx_flex_cell ltx_flex_size_2">
<figure id="S4.F15.sf6" class="ltx_figure ltx_figure_panel ltx_align_center"><object type="image/svg+xml" data="2509.07274v1/PerYear-Solidarity-FineGrained_Gruene.svg" id="S4.F15.sf6.g1" class="ltx_graphics ltx_centering ltx_img_landscape" style="aspect-ratio:476/201;" width="476" height="201"></object>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.sf6.3" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">(f)</span> </span><span id="S4.F15.sf6.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Die Grünen</span></figcaption>
</figure></div></div>
<figcaption class="ltx_caption ltx_centering"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_figure"><span id="S4.F15.4" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Figure 15</span>: </span><span id="S4.F15.5" class="ltx_text" style="font-size:90%;">Distribution of solidarity and anti-solidarity subtypes over time (2009–2025) for selected German parties. <span id="S4.F15.5.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">For each party, percentages are normalized within each year across all eight subtypes (thus, the curves in both panels together sum to 100% per year).</span> Note that AfD was first represented in the Bundestag in 2017, and the FDP was not represented from 2013 to 2017. Therefore, data points are absent for these parties during those respective periods.</span></figcaption>
</figure>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Fig. <a href="#S4.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 4.3.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> shows the raw numbers of migration-related statements per party and year, Fig. <a href="#S4.F15" title="Figure 15 ‣ 4.3.2 Distribution across parties ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">15</span></a> shows the fine-grained solidarity trends.</p>
</div>
<div id="S4.SS3.SSS2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<dl id="S4.I1" class="ltx_description">
<dt id="S4.I1.ix1" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The right-wing AfD</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">utters almost no expression of solidarity and the anti-solidarity subtypes remain relatively stable over time, with group-based anti-solidarity being consistently dominant (ca. 60% in 2018-2019 and ca. 75% in 2021-2025), although a notable number of samples with exchange-based solidarity (ca. 25%) occurs in the parliamentary period 2017-2021. Although the AfD is not the largest faction in parliament, they account for the largest share of anti-solidarity samples overall <span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(72.5%)</span>. <span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">The sharp rise of anti-solidarity after 2017 (Figure <a href="#S4.F13.sf1" title="In Figure 13 ‣ 4.3.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">13(a)</span></a>) aligns with the AfD’s entry into parliament and their rapid increase in migration-related utterances (Figure <a href="#S4.F14" title="Figure 14 ‣ 4.3.1 Overall solidarity distribution ‣ 4.3 Recent German history 2009-2025 ‣ 4 Temporal Trends in Solidarity Towards Migrants ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a>).</span> This can be explained by the fact that many AfD speeches, across topics, contain references to migrants illegally crossing the border, criminality of migrants, or migrants imposing additional burden on the German society leaving less resources to address the actual topic of legislative debate <span id="S4.I1.ix1.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 19 in Table <a href="#A3.T14" title="Table 14 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">14</span></a> in the Appendix where an AfD member argues that refugees displace elderly people from care homes).</span></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix2" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix2.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The center-right CDU/CSU and the liberal FDP</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">changed the most in our data. In the period 2009-2015, we observe a clear majority of solidarity samples, with a spike of compassionate solidarity for the CDU/CSU in 2014 (the FDP was not in parliament at that time). The main difference between both parties is more emphasis on exchange-based solidarity in the FDP, e.g. the contributions of incoming skilled workers to the German economy, motivating a new <span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Fachkräfteeinwanderungsgesetz</span> (<span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">en.</span>: skilled labor migration law) in 2023 <span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 20, where the FDP highlights the law as enabling foreign workers to contribute to the German economy).</span> However, for both parties, we observe a rise in group-based anti-solidarity, accounting for 80% of all samples in 2025. When inspecting the samples, the framing of migration is mostly in legalistic terms, such as regulating and controlling migration and reducing numbers, marking migrants as an outgroup, which is classified as group-based anti-solidarity by Llama-3.3-70B <span id="S4.I1.ix2.p1.1.4" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 21 where a CDU/CSU speaker frames migration as a threat to political stability and an excessive burden on local infrastructure, with calls for deportations, faster procedures, and rejections at the border; and Example 22 where an FDP speaker portrays asylum as being misused for state-subsidized residence).</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S4.I1.ix2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">The drastic shift toward group-based anti-solidarity over the last three years is insufficiently explained by recent literature. Partially, it may be that politicians from CDU/CSU and FDP take over populist rhetoric from AfD politicians who entered parliament since then <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx31" title="" class="ltx_ref">Esguerra et al., 2023</a>)</cite>. However,<cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx64" title="" class="ltx_ref">Lewandowsky et al. (2022)</a></cite> and <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx53" title="" class="ltx_ref">Kesting et al. (2018)</a></cite> did not observe effects of the AfD entering parliament on polarization, the use of populist language, or discourse quality. We also note that the first shift toward group-based anti-solidarity already occurred <em id="S4.I1.ix2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_emph ltx_font_italic">before</em> the AfD enters parliament in 2017. Alternatively, it may be that CDU/CSU and FDP respond to an overall right-wing shift in the German population <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zick et al., 2023</a>)</cite> by appealing to voters with anti-migration rhetoric and thereby providing a more moderate (but still anti-solidarity) alternative to the AfD. Overall, we cannot establish a full explanation but encourage further research into this striking and rapid trend.</p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix3" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix3.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The center-left SPD</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">also exhibits a spike in compassionate solidarity in 2014 and a slow decline in solidarity towards more group-based anti-solidarity (ca. 20% in 2024). Part of this is explained by mis-classifications of Llama-3.3-70B because SPD speakers often cite anti-solidarity stances of other parties (especially AfD) and these citations are mis-classified as anti-solidarity by the speaker <span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 23 where an SPD speaker cites AfD proposals putting foreigners under general suspicion in order to criticize them).</span> However, there are also samples where SPD speakers refer positively to their own policies having enabled deportations of declined asylum seekers or the difficulties associated with managing migration, similar to the frames of CDU/CSU and FDP speakers <span id="S4.I1.ix3.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 24).</span></p>
</div></dd>
<dt id="S4.I1.ix4" class="ltx_item"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_item"><span id="S4.I1.ix4.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">The left Green and Linke parties</span></span></dt>
<dd class="ltx_item">
<div id="S4.I1.ix4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S4.I1.ix4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">only display very few instances classified as anti-solidarity and almost all of them are misclassifications where Green or Linke speakers cite anti-solidarity statements or parliamentary motions by other parties <span id="S4.I1.ix4.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">(see Example 25 ).</span> These samples are nonetheless interesting because they express a polarizing influence of the AfD entering parliament, leading left-wing parties to positioning themselves in contrast <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx6" title="" class="ltx_ref">Atzpodien, 2022</a>)</cite>. Overall, Die Grünen initially emphasized compassionate solidarity, but later shifted to group-based appeals stressing integration and rights. In the 2024/25 campaign, their rhetoric focused strongly on exchange-based arguments about migrants’ economic and societal contributions <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx17" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bündnis 90/Die Grünen, 2025</a>)</cite> (see Example 26). Similarly, Die Linke began with predominantly compassionate appeals, framing migration as a humanitarian duty, but later emphasized migration as integral to society and advanced a detailed pro-immigration agenda, thereby increasing group-based solidarity. In the 2024/25 campaign, they moved further toward empathic solidarity, criticizing stereotyping and positioning themselves against exclusionary discourses <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx25" title="" class="ltx_ref">Die Linke, 2021</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx26" title="" class="ltx_ref">Die Linke, 2025</a>)</cite> (see Example 27). A special case is the BSW, which split off from the Linke party in 2024, partially due to explicit anti-migration positions. However, our data set contains only very few samples from this party (38 statements), so
we cannot provide a reliable quantitative analysis.</p>
</div></dd>
</dl>
</div>
</section>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S5" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">5 </span>Discussion and Conclusion</h2>

<div id="S5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In this paper, we have evaluated LLMs on the complex task of annotating (anti-)solidarity in German parliamentary speeches and interpreted the annotated data to derive new insights into (anti-)solidarity towards migrants in the post-war period as well as recent past. In more detail, we provide both methodological and sociological insights:</p>
</div>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Methodological perspective.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We find that instruction-tuned LLMs like <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.3-70B and GPT-4 are effective tools for annotating solidarity frames in parliamentary debates</span>, with open models offering a more cost-effective alternative to closed ones. In our experiments, Llama-3.3-70B performed competitively with GPT-4, especially in few-shot settings. Still, no model consistently outperformed GPT-4, and even GPT-4 did not fully attain the human upper bound. Existing research offers mixed evidence on the reliability of LLMs for annotation tasks: some highlight their limitations in handling subjective or expert-defined taxonomies <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx108" title="" class="ltx_ref">Ziems et al., 2024</a>)</cite>, while others show they can substitute or outperform human coders <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx43" title="" class="ltx_ref">Heseltine &amp; Clemm, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx98" title="" class="ltx_ref">Törnberg, 2024</a>)</cite>, or suggest that smaller, fine-tuned models are more reliable in political science applications <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx101" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wang et al., 2024</a>)</cite>. In our case, model performance generally improved with model size, but also varied by target group, historical period (with older data proving more difficult), and inference method. <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">These findings highlight the absence of a one-size-fits-all solution: rather than relying on a single model, both model and method selection must be adjusted to the requirements of the specific task.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1" class="ltx_p">Moreover, we emphasize <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">the need for validation at every stage of the analysis pipeline</span>. Even keyword search as a first filtering step can introduce biases in the data or can aggregate data that concerns very different sub-populations (such as different groups of migrants in the German post-war period). Regarding the LLM annotation itself, we validated model predictions against a large data set of human annotations (aggregating a year of labor involving multiple annotators), test multiple inference configurations (including zero- and few-shot prompting), and validate long-term trends produced by our two best-performing models (Llama-3.3-70B and GPT-4) against human annotations. We also conducted error analyses to identify where and why models fail to make sure that outputs can be reliably interpreted. This aligns with recent work advocating for human-in-the-loop workflows and partial human validation when using LLMs for annotation tasks, to ensure output quality and interpretability <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx74" title="" class="ltx_ref">Pangakis &amp; Wolken, 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx99" title="" class="ltx_ref">Wagner et al., 2025</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1" class="ltx_p">Despite these efforts, it remains essential to acknowledge model-specific weaknesses when interpreting results. For example, we observed that <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_font_bold">Llama-3.3-70B occasionally misclassifies anti-solidarity, particularly in instances where criticism of anti-migrant rhetoric is sometimes mistaken for anti-solidarity</span> framing itself. In such cases, models may overlook logical direction or target relevance and assign misleading labels despite otherwise coherent reasoning <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx106" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zhang et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. One possible reason for this behavior could be the influence of complex political language or long prompt context, which may distract the model from the intended target <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx48" title="" class="ltx_ref">Huang et al., 2025a</a>)</cite>. Importantly, we could only reveal model errors because we checked the primary data before drawing conclusions.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Social science perspective.</h5>

<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Beyond the methodological contributions, our study also demonstrates how an LLM-based pipeline can support a theoretically informed quantitative analysis through two illustrative case studies: one historical (1949–1957) and one contemporary (2009–2025).</p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1" class="ltx_p">For the early post-war period (1949–1957), we demonstrated how solidarity was shaped and institutionalized in the context of national reconstruction. While this era has been examined in previous historical research, our LLM-based, fine-grained analysis <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">offers additional insight for historical and social science research. First, it enables us to move beyond the assumption that solidarity during this period was simply <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“high”</span>: while historical consensus holds that it largely benefited ethnic Germans, our data reveal coexisting forms of (anti-)solidarity, and shifting rhetorical strategies across different migrant groups. Second, our approach helps integrate migration histories that are often treated separately, such as expellees, DPs, and refugees, into a shared analytical framework, allowing for direct comparison of how solidarity was articulated across groups. This also sheds light on the emerging distinction between early DPs and the broader category of <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Ausländer</span>, including those later identified as <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p2.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_font_italic">Gastarbeiter</span>, showing that solidarity was not uniformly absent but selectively expressed.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1" class="ltx_p">For the recent past (2009-2025), we see a striking shift from solidarity to anti-solidarity following the 2015 migration crisis. This may be partially explained by solidarity fatigue <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx23" title="" class="ltx_ref">De et al., 2023</a>)</cite> or may be part of a larger right-wing shift <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx107" title="" class="ltx_ref">Zick et al., 2023</a>)</cite> on migration issues. While stricter migration stances across Europe have been analyzed before, our study is the first to quantify the shift from solidarity to anti-solidarity in German parliament. Our data also revealed that the shift is most striking in the liberal and conservative parties, motivating further research. <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p3.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This development resonates with broader European trends, where populist radical right parties have increasingly mobilized around anti-immigration narratives <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx2" title="" class="ltx_ref">Aktas, 2024</a>)</cite>, and even mainstream conservative parties have shifted their positions on migration in response to the electoral pressure of right-wing populists <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx8" title="" class="ltx_ref">Bayerlein, 2021</a>)</cite>. Comparative survey evidence further shows that anti-immigrant attitudes remain a central driver of right-wing populist support across the continent <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx69" title="" class="ltx_ref">Messing &amp; Ságvári, 2021</a>)</cite>.</span></p>
</div>
<div id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">This type of fine-grained, longitudinal analysis of solidarity would hardly be feasible at such scale using manual annotation alone which, in our project, cost over €29,000 for only 3,500 instances. By contrast, with access to computing resources (e.g., in our case, in-house hardware with NVIDIA A40 GPUs), open-weight models such as Llama-3.3-70B can annotate tens of thousands of instances at minimal additional cost. Even closed models like GPT-4 are far cheaper, with one third of our dataset annotated for about €500. While machine annotation is therefore not <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“free”</span>, it is vastly more scalable than human annotation and enables large-scale analyses that would otherwise be financially prohibitive. In this sense, our method bridges <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1.2" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“distant reading”</span> approaches common in large-scale text analysis with elements of <span id="S5.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p4.1.1.3" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“close reading”</span>, enabling theoretically grounded systematic interpretation of parliamentary speech while retaining full corpus coverage.</span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
<section id="S6" class="ltx_section">
<h2 class="ltx_title ltx_title_section"><span class="ltx_tag ltx_tag_section">6 </span>Limitations and Future Work</h2>

<div id="S6.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.p1.1" class="ltx_p">While our analysis was performed carefully to avoid methodological pitfalls, we nonetheless acknowledge remaining limitations of our study.</p>
</div>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Human Label Variation:</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px1.p1.1" class="ltx_p">Even after months of training, discussion and improvements of the annotation manual, we still observe substantial disagreement between human annotators, especially on the fine-grained level. This indicates that (anti-)solidarity is intrinsically a challenging concept to annotate, especially on parliamentary speeches, and that reasonable disagreement may persist (e.g. whether anti-solidarity should be regarded as group-based or exchange-based when competition between groups around limited resources is highlighted). For this investigation, we used the majority label, but future work should consider methods that explicitly account for human label variation and embrace it <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx75" title="" class="ltx_ref">Plank, 2022</a>)</cite>.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Political Bias:</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1" class="ltx_p">It may be that the LLMs compared in this paper may be subject to political bias as investigated by several prior works <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citep">(<a href="#bib.bibx71" title="" class="ltx_ref">Motoki et al., 2024</a>; <a href="#bib.bibx84" title="" class="ltx_ref">Rettenberger et al., 2025</a>)</cite>. In particular, it may be that a left-wing political bias leads to more anti-solidarity being annotated. However, we do not observe a systematic bias toward anti-solidarity in the error analysis (Fig. <a href="#S3.F6" title="Figure 6 ‣ 3.2 Error Analysis ‣ 3 Results ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">6</span></a>); instead, models often mislabel solidarity and anti-solidarity samples as <span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px2.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text ltx_inline-quote ltx_outerquote">“None”</span>. It may also be that the human annotators are biased towards certain labels, but this would need to affect all annotators equally because we do not observe a systematic disagreement towards one label, either. Finally, even if bias were present, this would not explain differences and trends in (anti-)solidarity (which are the main subject of our analysis) but rather the overall level of (anti-)solidarity.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Analysis of (Anti-)solidarity towards women:</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px3.p1.1" class="ltx_p">In the same way we performed an analysis of (anti-)solidarity trends towards migrants, the (anti-)solidarity towards women could be analyzed. For example, recent work by <cite class="ltx_cite ltx_citemacro_citet"><a href="#bib.bibx24" title="" class="ltx_ref">Diabaté et al. (2023)</a></cite> suggests that there may be a shift to the left in Germany regarding gender, which would be an opposite trend compared to what we found regarding migration. Due to space and time constraints, such an analysis is not included in this work but appears highly promising for future work.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph">Further model variations:</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px4.p1.1" class="ltx_p">We considered a range of models as well as different prompts, and fine-tuning (for Llama models and BERT models) but a vast range of possible model variations remains to be explored, such as ensembles of models, less quantized models, newer models, etc. It may well be that human annotation quality can be fully matched with more refined tools.</p>
</div>
</section>
<section id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5" class="ltx_paragraph">
<h5 class="ltx_title ltx_title_paragraph" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Temporal Reliability:</h5>

<div id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1" class="ltx_para">
<p id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1" class="ltx_p"><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.1" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">Both model performance (macro F1) and human agreement vary over time, with notable dips in some decades (e.g., the 1960s for Llama-3.3-70B; Fig. </span><a href="#A3.F16" title="Figure 16 ‣ Fine-Tuned Llama Models ‣ Appendix C Models Inference and Training Details ‣ LLM Analysis of 150+ years of German Parliamentary Debates on Migration Reveals Shift from Post-War Solidarity to Anti-Solidarity in the Last Decade" class="ltx_ref" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;"><span class="ltx_text ltx_ref_tag">16</span></a><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.2" class="ltx_text" style="--ltx-fg-color:#000000;">). This reflects the difficulty of annotating historical language, but overall trend correlations between GPT-4, Llama-3.3-70B, and human annotations remain high. Since our analysis emphasizes broad temporal trends rather than isolated data points, these fluctuations do not undermine the validity of our conclusions.</span><span id="S6.SS0.SSS0.Px5.p1.1.3" class="ltx_text"></span></p>
</div>
</section>
</section>
